so a lot happening in in that  regard preparing for the arrival of  people who are fleeing the war in  ukraine an onslaught um thomas sparrow  thank you so much  now many of the people caught up in the  invasion have fled to neighboring  countries tens of thousands of  ukrainians have already left most are  headed to countries bordering ukraine  these include eu members poland slovakia  hungary romania as well as non-member  moldova as fighting worsens these  nations are now bracing for more  refugees in the coming days  the train station in the polish city of  shemeshol 10 kilometers from ukraine  for many ukrainian refugees it's their  first stop after crossing the border  these four children are having their  first hot meal in 30 hours after  traveling 450 kilometers through ukraine  their fathers weren't able to join them  they stayed behind to fight the children  are now waiting for their mothers to  arrive  there were masses of people on the way  we took a bus towards the border and  then walked then we stood in a long  queue  suddenly we saw our cousins and we've  been together since then  we waited a long time for the transport  from the border  and now we're finally here  the way was hard and long  because of traffic jams they had to get  off the bus and walk the last 15  kilometers to the border  near leve they heard explosions  it was terrible there were rockets  flying  we came here without passports we only  have our birth certificates we were let  into poland as refugees  the polish authorities have decided to  let all ukrainians into the country even  those who have no passports which are  normally required to enter the eu  at the train station as well as in  several reception centers they are  supported by volunteers and the military  the refugees are relieved to have  escaped the war  but they're also worried about what they  have left behind the events in their  country  those who are not picked up here by  friends or relatives are taken in by  private individuals  the willingness to help is great  within a day of the russian invasion  thirty thousand refugees arrived in  poland from ukraine the government in  warsaw expects that up to one million  people could flee to the country and it  promises that they will all be taken in  let's bring in now dw correspondent max  zander who is at a the train station in  shemeshol  max more than a hundred thousand  ukrainian refugees have already arrived  in poland  describe the situation there  well we're at the train station here in  chemistry as you rightly pointed out and  it just became a lot more busy um over  the last last few minutes uh the last  train from ukraine just came in and  brought a lot of new people and people  here actually uh polish locals and  people from the city council  firefighters as well as soldiers are  working very hard to provide provide  help provide immediate donations like  these food donations over here and  services to the people  here who arrive here at the airport so  they're handling out food but they're  also helping them with their next travel  arrangements um trying to  bring them together with family and  relatives if there are some in poland or  neighboring countries or if not help  them with accommodation bring them to  some of the reception centers that have  been put in place uh here in the country  and the interior minister deputy  interior minister of poland uh came to  the area this morning and spoke to the  press and said i pointed out more than a  hundred thousand people um already have  crossed from ukraine to poland and  they're likely going to be a lot more um  numbers conservative estimates were  about 1 million but perhaps they're  going to be even more than that  but it seems that authorities are and  locals are doing their uttermost to to  provide assistance to the people who  have come here already  max it's literally buzzing there i can't  imagine that there's going to be a let  up anytime soon the refugees are often  on the road for hours i imagine they  arrive having not slept or eaten much  what condition are they in  that's absolutely right i mean people  people are leaving ukraine for a reason  people are anxious people have fear  people have experienced trauma perhaps  but also  they are just very exhausted the trip  through ukraine and then also the last  lake crossing the border um takes takes  a lot of time the land crossings um have  been particularly difficult uh the car  crossings um because there have been a  huge pileups of traffic people had to  leave their cars and and walk the last  few kilometers to cross the border so  people who arrived there are naturally  very exhausted um in addition to that uh  the ukrainian authorities um  made it uh  changed the the the crossings the style  of the crossings that you cannot cross  by car um from ukraine to poland any  longer because a lot of the drivers were  male and uh they're trying to keep  anybody at the fighting age between 18  and 60 in the country so now um people  who leave ukraine and cross the border  to poland actually have to walk or if  they come by train  here to the train station it's also very  difficult um because you're dealing with  a lot of checks  for for the same reason and a lot of  delays so people here are  in a very difficult state  max i wonder how do people on the ground  in poland actually view this attack in  ukraine um are they are they angry or is  there fear that perhaps russia may may  have them in its sights  i mean that's that's difficult to say i  mean everybody  everybody uh  across the board is is worried um that's  for sure um there  have has been uh  his historic um  uh mistrust to uh  to to to russia for especially some  older parts of the polish generation um  so people are viewing very close what's  happening and a lot of people are afraid  that this that violence may spill over  and that perhaps um being in the  european union and being a nato might  not be enough protection after all but  at the moment uh the situation is it's  very fast moving of course and people  are obviously worried and watching very  close to what's happening  max zander with a group of very tired  and hungry people there in shemeshol  many many thanks  dw correspondent funny fachar joins us  on the line and actually we see pictures  of her there  she is she was in kiev but is no longer  there has moved to another location  altogether uh funny tell us where you  are and uh what's going on around you  i mean kamenetzky which is about  normally about four hours from kiev it  actually took us 14 hours yesterday to  get here and what you actually see  behind me is normally a theater but it's  no longer theater but it has become a  refugee center for internally displaced  people from all all regions of ukraine  trying to find a shelter here now on the  other side just across from this shelter  you see a long long line of people  volunteers a man of all ages trying to  sign up to help ukraine defend itself  what's happening really here in  hamanetsky is something it also also  tells you  what the situation right now is in  ukraine and that is a combination of  panic a combination of  and and and still i hope though that it  may not go as far as as a lot of people  are worried that it may that this is  going to be a war for a long long long  time to come people are hoping that  there is going to be a negotiation  between ukraine and russia to prevent  the death and the further bloodshed  among civilians among soldiers and  everyone involved here in this situation  in ukraine  and funny as we saw those images of  people who are now displaced you get the  sense that this is certainly just the  beginning um what have you heard from  ukraine's capital kiev  what's happening in keefe is is  basically just a big mess right now  people are panicking they are trying to  find shelter go to the metro station the  mayor of kiev declared that all metros  are going to not are not going to  operate any longer they are only  operating as bunkers as shelters now we  have seen of course  heart-wrenching pictures of children  huddling together scared and it is a  very very critical situation as we are  hearing that the  russian military troops are building up  around the city  apparently only 30 kilometers from the  city center there's a heavy russian  troop built up but already there are  russian soldiers in on the ground in  kiev so it's expected that street fights  between ukrainian and russian forces are  about to begin or even to escalate at  the same time the president president  zielenski appeared in a video on social  media early this morning basically  trying to show i'm still here i'm not  going to lay down arms uh because he  said rumors have it that people think  that he is going to urge people to lay  down their arms and dimitri  militarize as it's being expected by a  russian president vladimir putin so a  lot of chaos and in fact what is  engulfing right now keith and it's  really unclear what the next hours are  going to bring there just like it's  unclear what's going to happen to the  rest of the country and there's so many  explosions across the country in  various regions even though the most  intense shelling so far still is in the  east and in the southern and in the  northern parts of ukraine  okay that's uh dw's funny for char many  many thanks funny and please stay safe  you're watching dw news here's the main  story we're following for you  recapping the latest developments uh  ukraine's military says it repelled an  assault by russian troops on an army  base in vassal kev about 40 kilometers  south of kiev the mayor says there were  heavy casualties russian news agency  interfax reports that its forces have  taken control of the ukrainian city of  melatopal if confirmed this would be the  first major city to fall street fighting  continues in the capital kiev intense  gunfire and explosions rocked the city  overnight  a missile has struck an apartment block  in a kiev suburb that russian forces  were attempting to capture russia says  it is firing rockets and cruise missiles
 

